{"name":"Mikado","hex":"#3f3623","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mikado","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A hamlet in Saskatchewan, Canada.","A township and unincorporated community therein, in Alcona County, Michigan, United States."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of mikado (“emperor of Japan”).","The steam locomotive 2-8-2 wheel arrangement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mikado"}],"entries":[{"title":"Emperor of Japan","blocks":[{"text":"The Emperor of Japan is the hereditary monarch and head of state of Japan. The emperor is defined by the Constitution of Japan as the symbol of the Japanese state and the unity of the Japanese people, his position deriving from \"the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power\". The Imperial Household Law governs the line of imperial succession. Pursuant to his constitutional role as a national symbol, and in accordance with rulings by the Supreme Court of Japan, the emperor is personally immune from prosecution. By virtue of his position as the head of the Imperial House, the emperor is also recognized as the head of the Shinto religion, which holds him to be the direct descendant of the sun goddess Amaterasu. According to tradition, the office of emperor was created in the 7th century BC, but the first historically verifiable emperors appear around the 3rd or 4th centuries AD."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Flag_of_the_Japanese_Emperor.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Japanese_Emperor.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_of_Japan","wikidata":"Q208233"},{"title":"The Mikado","blocks":[{"text":"The Mikado; or, The Town of Titipu is a comic opera in two acts, with music by Arthur Sullivan and libretto by W. S. Gilbert, their ninth of fourteen operatic collaborations. It opened on 14 March 1885, in London, where it ran at the Savoy Theatre for 672 performances, the second-longest run for any work of musical theatre and one of the longest runs of any theatre piece up to that time. By the end of 1885, it was estimated that, in Europe and America, at least 150 companies were producing the opera."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Doyly_Carte_Opera_Company_poster%2C_John_Hassall.jpg/330px-Doyly_Carte_Opera_Company_poster%2C_John_Hassall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mikado","wikidata":"Q1195818"},{"title":"Mikado (game)","blocks":[{"text":"Mikado is a pick-up sticks game of European origin, played with a set of same-length sticks which can measure between 17 and 20 cm. One of the oldest preserved sets of Mikado game sticks dates back to the 17th century. The game's origins are likely Eastern, as evidenced by the existence of preserved game sets from that region."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikado_(game)","wikidata":"Q1333813"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg/500px-Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg/1280px-Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg/500px-Emperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmperor_Naruhito_201905.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/20101129gikai1.jpg/500px-20101129gikai1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/201011